{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20261115014022c6ca161a6f5640bf","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"claude-opus-4-8","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes. When you point me at a `.rs` file (or a module, crate, or diff), I first determine the scope and context — library vs. application, edition, MSRV, and any frameworks like Bevy — then run `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features`, and `cargo test` if a toolchain is available. I then review the code by hand in priority order: correctness and soundness (UB, reachable panics, trait-contract violations, lock/await hazards), error handling, API design, idiom and clarity, and docs/tests. The output is a prioritized report — **Must fix / Should fix / Consider / What's already good** — where every finding cites the rule it breaks (e.g. a Rust API Guidelines code or clippy lint) with a concrete, minimal code fix; I review rather than rewrite, unless you ask me to change the code."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8927ccb7-cd93-4170-ac97-95095f8a46a3","uuid":"4f69e97b-1e87-433b-882a-ca58c55dfec7"}
